Each kind maps to a dimension (e.g. Freshness → Timeliness).

How to add a monitor (for admins)
Who: Administrators only (API: RequireAdmin; UI: New monitor only when role === admin).

Where: Data Trust → top right New monitor (also Run checks to execute all active rules).

Steps (same as the dialog):

Name (≥ 3 characters) and optional description.
Kind (see table above).
Dataset and column (from catalog — unknown columns are rejected).
Severity and target (threshold 0–100% pass rate).
Owner (e.g. Data Engineering) and optional tags.
Submit → API POST /api/v1/trust/reliability/monitors → monitor is saved and included in the next run.
After creation: use Run checks (or wait for cached refresh) to see status in the rules catalog and impact on the trust score.

Admin Center → Data Trust Admin is complementary: enable/disable built-in DQ rules and refresh trust scores platform-wide, without building a new custom monitor.

Leadership examples (storytelling)
Example 1 — Maruti / automotive sales
Goal: Ensure AI Chat and revenue views are not using stale sales.

Built-in: Freshness on fact_sales (SLA-style; already in defaults).
Custom monitor (admin adds):
Kind: Freshness
Dataset: Sales transactions
Max age: 36 hours
Severity: High
Target: 100%
If it fails: Timeliness dimension drops; hero shows Risk/Fair; rule detail explains lag; assets list includes AI Chat answers so leaders know NLQ is affected.
Example 2 — Insurance claims
Goal: No claims with invalid status breaking loss-ratio KPIs.

Custom monitor:
Kind: Allowed values
Dataset: Claims
Column: claim_status
Allowed: Open, Closed, Pending, …
Dimension: Validity
Target: 99%
If it fails: Validity fails; drill-down shows sample bad values; executives see trust drop before trusting claim dashboards.
Example 3 — Dealer consistency
Goal: Sales rows must point to real dealers.

Custom monitor:
Kind: Reference match
Dataset: Sales
Column: dealer_id
Reference: Dealers dataset, same column
Dimension: Consistency
Helps: Prevents “orphan” sales that inflate regional rollups and confuse chat answers that filter by dealer.
What leaders should remember
Monitors = automated promises about data (“fresh enough,” “complete,” “consistent”).
Built-in rules cover industry-critical reconciliations; custom monitors let the business add their policies without writing SQL.
Trust score is the rollup for steering meetings; failing critical monitors mean “slow down decisions on that data until fixed.”
Only admins create/delete custom monitors; all users can view Data Trust to see transparency.
Fixing data or the pipeline improves the score on the next Run checks; disabling a rule hides the signal but does not fix the underlying issue.
Optional one-liner for the board
“Data Trust monitors are like smoke detectors on our analytics warehouse—they run every day, feed one trust score, and tell us if dashboards and Ask DB are safe to use before we act on the numbers.”

